From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 10:17:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Move the key generator into the backend

Script kept each store's key generator: every IDBObjectStore handle
cached the current number when it was created, generated keys and
applied "possibly update the key generator" itself, sent the new number
along with each put, and restored its cached number when a transaction
aborted. Two handles of one store, in different connections or
transactions, could hand out the same key, and the arithmetic ran in
f64, where 2^53 + 1 rounds to 2^53, so an explicit key of 2^53 or more
didn't use the generator up and the overflow rules failed.

Now the backend owns the key generator (design decision 4). The current
number stays in the object_store row; put_record, the backend's "store
a record into an object store", generates a key when the put has none
(failing above 2^53 with the new PutItemResult::KeyGeneratorExhausted,
which script reports as a ConstraintError) and otherwise runs "possibly
update the key generator" in i64: the key clamped to 2^53, floored, and
the current number moved past it if it is not below. The add check
comes before any write, and the new current number is written after the
record, in the transaction's SQL transaction, so a failed put changes
nothing, the key generator included, as the spec's key generator
section requires, and an abort's ROLLBACK reverts it. Script keeps the
synchronous checks of add/put, including "check that a key could be
injected into a value", and sends no key when the backend has to
generate one; its key generator state, the abort snapshot of it, the
field in PutItem and the current number in the object store metadata
are gone.

A store with a key generator and an in-line key path stores a value
under a generated key without that key (design decision 5): script no
longer knows the key before the put replies. GetItem and GetAllItems
now reply with IndexedDBValue, the serialized value and its record's
primary key (Iterate's records had it already), and
IDBObjectStore::deserialize_value, which get, getAll and cursor values
all use, injects the primary key with "inject a key into a value using
a key path" when evaluating the key path on the deserialized value
fails and the object the key would attach to, and every object before
it on the key path, is an ordinary Object or an Array: the put
evaluated the same path on an equivalent value, and storing keeps an
injected property only on those, so that is exactly when the stored
value lost a generated key. A Date, Map, Blob, typed array or other
exotic object stored under a generated key comes back without it, as
before this change and as the spec stores it. Values with the key
already in them keep what they have, including explicit keys of a type
that converting the key back would change (a typed array comes back as
an ArrayBuffer), and so do the values stored before this change, which
contain their generated key; the record format is unchanged, so the
schema version stays.

Tests on every executor: generated and explicit keys, including
strings, dates, arrays, fractions and negative numbers; generation up
to 2^53 and its failure after; explicit keys of 2^53 and more, up to
Infinity, and large negative ones; an add of an existing key changes
nothing and the transaction goes on; an abort reverts what generated
and explicit keys did to the key generator. A fork WPT test,
tests/wpt/mozilla/tests/IndexedDB/generated-key-value-kinds.any.js,
checks which values come back with a generated key: plain objects and
arrays, also nested, but not Dates, Maps, Sets, Blobs, Files,
ArrayBuffers, typed arrays, RegExps, Boolean objects or a nested path
through a Date.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits` at this commit: 102 passed, 1 skipped (the ignored long test; before: 97). DAYU200 at the tip of the series, `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage -- [--no-default-features] --features ohos-rdb-backend --status-level all`: RDB only 106 passed, 3 skipped; both backends 182 passed, 4 skipped (the skips are the documented test_new_storage_threads_create_independent_groups and the ignored long tests). WPT IndexedDB/ at the tip of the series (with the P1 review fixes), passing subtests of 4,511: host SpiderMonkey 3,251 with no unexpected result, host QuickJS 3,253, DAYU200 in the board configuration (QuickJS + RDB) 3,247 (before the series 3,224, 3,229 and 3,222). New passes in all three, when first measured: the 7 keygenerator.any.js subtests on explicit keys of 2^53 and more and on the overflow, in the window, worker and sharedworker globals, 10 of 10 repeats on both host engines. /_mozilla/IndexedDB/generated-key-value-kinds.any.js: 20 of its 22 window and worker subtests failed with injection for every object, 22 of 22 pass on both host engines, and the board passes all its globals. no new failures: QuickJS idbversionchangeevent.any.sharedworker timed out once and passed 20 of 20 on both host engines, and the board's idbversionchangeevent.any.worker saw a blocked event from the sharedworker variant, which uses the same database name and whose connection deleteDatabase doesn't close yet; the other differences are known flips. Values stored by the previous build: a database written by the P1.1 QuickJS build (generated keys in a single-segment and a nested key path, an explicit key) reads back the same through get, getAll and a cursor as with the old build, and a put stores the value without its key and reads it back with the key injected; checked before the review fixes, which change only exotic values.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent wrote the change and its tests, and ran the unit tests and WPT on the host and the DAYU200; another agent session restricted the injection to ordinary objects and Arrays and added the WPT test for the P1 review)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
